	<body>
		<segment id="1" parent="175" relname="joint-sequence">All rise</segment>
		<segment id="2" parent="175" relname="joint-sequence">and give your attention to the honorable justices of the Montana Supreme Court .</segment>
		<segment id="3" parent="198" relname="span">Now in session .</segment>
		<segment id="4" parent="177" relname="organization-phatic">Good morning everybody .</segment>
		<segment id="5" parent="177" relname="span">This is the time</segment>
		<segment id="6" parent="176" relname="span">set for oral argument in cause number DA 23-0716 .</segment>
		<segment id="7" parent="6" relname="elaboration-additional">Rodney Brandt and others versus R &amp; R Mountain Escapes LLC .</segment>
		<segment id="8" parent="9" relname="attribution-positive">So I , uh , understand</segment>
		<segment id="9" parent="174" relname="span">Mr. Frampton will be arguing for the the appellants .</segment>
		<segment id="10" parent="258" relname="topic-question">Is that correct ?</segment>
		<segment id="11" parent="12" relname="organization-phatic">Sorry .</segment>
		<segment id="12" parent="258" relname="span">Appellees .</segment>
		<segment id="13" parent="202" relname="joint-list">And Miss Baucus will be arguing for the appellants .</segment>
		<segment id="14" parent="173" relname="attribution-positive">And then I have a note here</segment>
		<segment id="15" parent="173" relname="span">that you want to split your time ,</segment>
		<segment id="16" parent="15" relname="mode-manner">uh , saving 15 minutes for rebuttal .</segment>
		<segment id="17" parent="18" relname="topic-question">Is that accurate ?</segment>
		<segment id="18" parent="260" relname="span">Yep , that 'd be fine .</segment>
		<segment id="19" parent="20" relname="organization-phatic">All right .</segment>
		<segment id="20" parent="206" relname="span">Ready to proceed .</segment>
		<segment id="21" parent="203" relname="organization-preparation">May it please the court .</segment>
		<segment id="22" parent="265" relname="organization-phatic">And good morning .</segment>
		<segment id="23" parent="266" relname="span">I 'm Stephanie Baucus here on behalf of the appellants .</segment>
		<segment id="24" parent="169" relname="same-unit">R &amp; R Mountain Escapes ,</segment>
		<segment id="25" parent="26" relname="organization-phatic">uh who are --</segment>
		<segment id="26" parent="204" relname="span">it 's completely owned by Doctor Russell Palmer and Ramona Stewart .</segment>
		<segment id="27" parent="265" relname="joint-list">Ramona Stewart is here with us today .</segment>
		<segment id="28" parent="178" relname="span">Also here are amicus parties</segment>
		<segment id="29" parent="28" relname="elaboration-attribute">who have filed briefs in support of the Palmers ,</segment>
		<segment id="30" parent="178" relname="elaboration-additional">including the Landlords ' Association and the Bridger Canyon Property Rights Coalition .</segment>
		<segment id="31" parent="293" relname="span">Thank you</segment>
		<segment id="32" parent="294" relname="span">for inviting us here</segment>
		<segment id="33" parent="295" relname="span">to address these important issues for our state ,</segment>
		<segment id="34" parent="33" relname="elaboration-attribute">which involve important questions of contract interpretation , the scope of property rights , and contract construction .</segment>
		<segment id="35" parent="296" relname="joint-list">Not only are these questions important for the Palmers and the plaintiffs ,</segment>
		<segment id="36" parent="197" relname="span">they are important for Montana homeowners and tenants</segment>
		<segment id="37" parent="196" relname="span">because residential homes in our state , most of them have some form of residential covenants</segment>
		<segment id="38" parent="37" relname="elaboration-attribute">that apply to them .</segment>
		<segment id="39" parent="222" relname="organization-preparation">I 'd like to emphasize three main points in my argument today .</segment>
		<segment id="40" parent="170" relname="span">First , there are two undisputed facts in this case</segment>
		<segment id="41" parent="40" relname="elaboration-attribute">that resolve the case in appellants ' favor .</segment>
		<segment id="42" parent="208" relname="joint-list">First , the covenants allow rentals ,</segment>
		<segment id="43" parent="208" relname="joint-list">and second , the covenants have no language about duration .</segment>
		<segment id="44" parent="45" relname="attribution-positive">We contend</segment>
		<segment id="45" parent="207" relname="span">that this makes the covenants unambiguous .</segment>
		<segment id="46" parent="210" relname="span">Second , our theory of the case is consistent with precedent ,</segment>
		<segment id="47" parent="209" relname="span">including Craig Tracts and its progeny like Meyers v Kleinhans ,</segment>
		<segment id="48" parent="47" relname="adversative-concession">whether or not the court finds the covenants ambiguous .</segment>
		<segment id="49" parent="213" relname="span">And third , this case is critical to upholding Montana 's historic protection of fundamental property rights .</segment>
		<segment id="50" parent="211" relname="joint-list">Not only is rental use one of the sticks in the bundle of property rights ,</segment>
		<segment id="51" parent="211" relname="joint-list">but short term rental use has been around since the founding of our country .</segment>
		<segment id="52" parent="298" relname="same-unit">And</segment>
		<segment id="53" parent="54" relname="contingency-condition">if the district court 's decision is not reversed ,</segment>
		<segment id="54" parent="188" relname="span">this case could have wide sweeping and destructive impacts for thousands of Montana homeowners in the rental context and otherwise .</segment>
		<segment id="55" parent="216" relname="organization-preparation">Upon the first point , there are two critical and disputed facts .</segment>
		<segment id="56" parent="57" relname="attribution-positive">First , we think</segment>
		<segment id="57" parent="189" relname="span">these facts make the covenants unambiguous .</segment>
		<segment id="58" parent="299" relname="span">But at a minimum , they create an ambiguity</segment>
		<segment id="59" parent="58" relname="elaboration-attribute">that must be resolved in appellant 's favor .</segment>
		<segment id="60" parent="270" relname="span">Unlike the court 's recent decisions in Craig Tracts or Brown -- Craig Tracts v Brown Drake or Meyers v Kleinhans , in this case , the restrictive covenants at issue permit rentals .</segment>
		<segment id="61" parent="268" relname="span">The terms expressly contemplate rentals in the section A6 ,</segment>
		<segment id="62" parent="61" relname="elaboration-attribute">which is about advertising ,</segment>
		<segment id="63" parent="269" relname="span">and it 's talking about signs .</segment>
		<segment id="64" parent="63" relname="elaboration-additional">It allows signs , advertising billboards , and advertising structures for sale or rent .</segment>
		<segment id="65" parent="215" relname="joint-list">And the plaintiffs themselves use their property for long term rental .</segment>
		<segment id="66" parent="67" relname="topic-question">Could I interrupt you ?</segment>
		<segment id="67" parent="171" relname="span">Of course .</segment>
		<segment id="68" parent="69" relname="context-background">So the covenants also say only for residential purposes , not commercial .</segment>
		<segment id="69" parent="218" relname="span">Um , how do you address that ?</segment>
		<segment id="70" parent="71" relname="adversative-concession">I understand your argument with respect to the rentals ,</segment>
		<segment id="71" parent="301" relname="span">but the covenants also say not for anything commercial .</segment>
		<segment id="72" parent="301" relname="elaboration-additional">And Meyers .</segment>
		<segment id="73" parent="225" relname="organization-phatic">So , Your Honor , thank you for the question .</segment>
		<segment id="74" parent="303" relname="attribution-positive">Um , we certainly contend</segment>
		<segment id="75" parent="181" relname="span">that the short term rentals</segment>
		<segment id="76" parent="75" relname="elaboration-attribute">that the Palmers have been doing ,</segment>
		<segment id="77" parent="181" relname="elaboration-attribute">um , which were a minimum five night stay ,</segment>
		<segment id="78" parent="180" relname="span">the Palmers themselves stayed in the property for eight weeks of the year , um , 27 nights maximum</segment>
		<segment id="79" parent="78" relname="elaboration-attribute">that they had rented out in 23 .</segment>
		<segment id="80" parent="184" relname="same-unit">Um , those uses ,</segment>
		<segment id="81" parent="82" relname="context-circumstance">when the renters are there ,</segment>
		<segment id="82" parent="272" relname="span">are the same</segment>
		<segment id="83" parent="185" relname="span">as they would be</segment>
		<segment id="84" parent="83" relname="context-circumstance">when the Palmers are there .</segment>
		<segment id="85" parent="172" relname="joint-list">They 're eating</segment>
		<segment id="86" parent="172" relname="joint-list">and bathing</segment>
		<segment id="87" parent="172" relname="joint-list">and sleeping ,</segment>
		<segment id="88" parent="172" relname="joint-list">recreating ,</segment>
		<segment id="89" parent="172" relname="joint-list">relaxing ,</segment>
		<segment id="90" parent="172" relname="joint-list">um , potentially doing a little bit --</segment>
		<segment id="91" parent="273" relname="span">How is that not commercial ?</segment>
		<segment id="92" parent="93" relname="organization-phatic">I mean ,</segment>
		<segment id="93" parent="226" relname="span">we found in Kleinhans -- Kleinhans</segment>
		<segment id="94" parent="228" relname="span">that short term rentals are commercial use</segment>
		<segment id="95" parent="227" relname="span">that violate a covenant ,</segment>
		<segment id="96" parent="95" relname="elaboration-attribute">that says no commercial use .</segment>
		<segment id="97" parent="98" relname="attribution-positive">So I know</segment>
		<segment id="98" parent="230" relname="span">that we have the rental covenant .</segment>
		<segment id="99" parent="232" relname="joint-list">Then we have the no commercial use covenant .</segment>
		<segment id="100" parent="231" relname="span">How do you weave them together</segment>
		<segment id="101" parent="100" relname="purpose-goal">to give both of them meaning ?</segment>
		<segment id="102" parent="235" relname="organization-phatic">Right .</segment>
		<segment id="103" parent="234" relname="span">Well , so the definitions</segment>
		<segment id="104" parent="103" relname="elaboration-attribute">that the court looked at</segment>
		<segment id="105" parent="276" relname="same-unit">and the set of covenants in Meyers v Kleinhans were different</segment>
		<segment id="106" parent="276" relname="adversative-antithesis">than they are in this case .</segment>
		<segment id="107" parent="237" relname="span">In this case , we have a specific set of provisions ,</segment>
		<segment id="108" parent="236" relname="span">one of which is to expressly allow rentals</segment>
		<segment id="109" parent="108" relname="mode-manner">without any reference to duration .</segment>
		<segment id="110" parent="305" relname="same-unit">And so</segment>
		<segment id="111" parent="304" relname="span">if you 're going to read the covenant and the contract as a whole ,</segment>
		<segment id="112" parent="111" relname="elaboration-additional">which is required under standard principles of contract instruction -- contract construction ,</segment>
		<segment id="113" parent="186" relname="span">you have to do that in a way</segment>
		<segment id="114" parent="113" relname="elaboration-attribute">that makes those provisions harmonize .</segment>
		<segment id="115" parent="308" relname="same-unit">And</segment>
		<segment id="116" parent="117" relname="purpose-goal">in order to do that ,</segment>
		<segment id="117" parent="306" relname="span">you need to look at what the drafter intended by the definition of commercial .</segment>
		<segment id="118" parent="280" relname="span">And so the definitions</segment>
		<segment id="119" parent="118" relname="elaboration-attribute">that this court looked to in the Meyers v Kleinhans case</segment>
		<segment id="120" parent="279" relname="same-unit">would also prohibit long term rentals in this case .</segment>
		<segment id="121" parent="122" relname="attribution-positive">And we know</segment>
		<segment id="122" parent="238" relname="span">that that 's not going to be possible</segment>
		<segment id="123" parent="239" relname="span">because there is a provision</segment>
		<segment id="124" parent="123" relname="elaboration-attribute">that contemplates rentals .</segment>
		<segment id="125" parent="309" relname="span">So the type of commercial activities are further defined by the drafter 's intent .</segment>
		<segment id="126" parent="242" relname="span">You can see in the provision</segment>
		<segment id="127" parent="126" relname="elaboration-attribute">regarding commercial use</segment>
		<segment id="128" parent="242" relname="context-circumstance">where it talks about ' such as '</segment>
		<segment id="129" parent="130" relname="attribution-positive">it says ,</segment>
		<segment id="130" parent="243" relname="span">that commercial uses are prohibited ,</segment>
		<segment id="131" parent="313" relname="span">it says " such as junkyards and mobile home parks " .</segment>
		<segment id="132" parent="281" relname="span">But it also says " etc. "</segment>
		<segment id="133" parent="132" relname="organization-phatic">Right ?</segment>
		<segment id="134" parent="241" relname="span">Correct .</segment>
		<segment id="135" parent="315" relname="span">And also it says " whatsoever " .</segment>
		<segment id="136" parent="282" relname="span">So I mean no commercial use whatsoever .</segment>
		<segment id="137" parent="283" relname="span">It seems very broad to me .</segment>
		<segment id="138" parent="139" relname="organization-phatic">I mean ,</segment>
		<segment id="139" parent="244" relname="span">to get the intention .</segment>
		<segment id="140" parent="141" relname="attribution-positive">The appellants do n't disagree</segment>
		<segment id="141" parent="284" relname="span">that the commercial use prohibition is somewhat broad ,</segment>
		<segment id="142" parent="285" relname="span">but it ca n't be read to prohibit rentals .</segment>
		<segment id="143" parent="245" relname="attribution-positive">The plaintiffs and the defendants or appellants and appellees both agree</segment>
		<segment id="144" parent="245" relname="joint-list">that long term rentals are permitted um under the covenants ,</segment>
		<segment id="145" parent="245" relname="joint-list">and there 's no difference between a rental of 28 days and a rental of 31 days .</segment>
		<segment id="146" parent="246" relname="span">The court in the district um the district court pulled a 30 day limitation out ,</segment>
		<segment id="147" parent="146" relname="elaboration-additional">and it created that limitation onto the contract .</segment>
		<segment id="148" parent="247" relname="span">There is nothing in the contract</segment>
		<segment id="149" parent="148" relname="elaboration-attribute">that talks about that .</segment>
		<segment id="150" parent="248" relname="span">Um , and again , the rental use</segment>
		<segment id="151" parent="291" relname="span">that is being done by the guests or the renters</segment>
		<segment id="152" parent="151" relname="elaboration-attribute">that the Palmers have at their property ,</segment>
		<segment id="153" parent="250" relname="span">um , was the same</segment>
		<segment id="154" parent="249" relname="span">as it would be</segment>
		<segment id="155" parent="154" relname="adversative-concession">no matter what the duration was .</segment>
		<segment id="156" parent="194" relname="span">Let me follow up on that ,</segment>
		<segment id="157" parent="191" relname="attribution-positive">because I think</segment>
		<segment id="158" parent="191" relname="span">they they make the point</segment>
		<segment id="159" parent="320" relname="span">that that 's regulatory</segment>
		<segment id="160" parent="159" relname="causal-cause">based on the county 's permit .</segment>
		<segment id="161" parent="162" relname="organization-phatic">And I 'll ,</segment>
		<segment id="162" parent="190" relname="span">we 'll address that later .</segment>
		<segment id="163" parent="321" relname="span">But I want to go back to what Justice Gustafson was talking about .</segment>
		<segment id="164" parent="193" relname="span">So the prohibition is any other commercial ?</segment>
		<segment id="165" parent="287" relname="adversative-antithesis">It does n't say use .</segment>
		<segment id="166" parent="167" relname="attribution-positive">It says</segment>
		<segment id="167" parent="287" relname="span">purpose , " prohibits any other commercial purpose whatsoever . "</segment>
		<segment id="168" parent="288" relname="evaluation-comment">So whatsoever is a pretty broad term .</segment>
		<group id="169" type="multinuc" parent="23" relname="elaboration-attribute"/>
		<group id="170" type="span" parent="208" relname="organization-preparation"/>
		<group id="171" type="span" parent="219" relname="organization-preparation"/>
